WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:18.570
Our next speaker
will be Kiran Keshav.

00:00:18.570 --> 00:00:22.180
Kiran is the Executive Director
of the Yale Center for Research

00:00:22.180 --> 00:00:24.840
Computing and the Senior
Director of Research

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:26.760
Technologies at Yale.

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:29.370
Kiran joined the
staff of Yale in 2013

00:00:29.370 --> 00:00:33.180
as ITS Director of Research
Services, and, in 2014,

00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:36.810
he was appointed Senior
Director of Research Technology.

00:00:36.810 --> 00:00:39.090
Before coming to
Yale, Kiran served

00:00:39.090 --> 00:00:43.020
as Chief Technology Officer and
Vice President of Technology

00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:46.140
at Betterment LLC
in New York City.

00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:49.410
Prior to this, Kiran was the
Manager of the Development

00:00:49.410 --> 00:00:53.610
Group at the Columbia University
Center for Bioinformatics.

00:00:53.610 --> 00:00:55.170
Kiran holds an
undergraduate degree

00:00:55.170 --> 00:00:58.800
from Queens University in
Canada and an Executive Master

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:01.260
of Science degree in technology
management from Columbia

00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:02.190
University.

00:01:02.190 --> 00:01:04.330
And he is truly our host today.

00:01:04.330 --> 00:01:06.125
So welcome, Kiran.

00:01:06.125 --> 00:01:07.619
[APPLAUSE]

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:46.924
Can you hear me?

00:01:46.924 --> 00:01:47.590
Can you hear me?

00:01:50.590 --> 00:01:52.269
Thanks, Steve.

00:01:52.269 --> 00:01:53.810
Now I'm just thinking,
how am I going

00:01:53.810 --> 00:01:57.320
to implement these things?

00:01:57.320 --> 00:02:01.132
[INAUDIBLE] an opportunity
this is [INAUDIBLE]

00:02:01.132 --> 00:02:03.825
how we are going to show how
open science should be done.

00:02:03.825 --> 00:02:04.325
Exactly.

00:02:04.325 --> 00:02:04.866
There you go.

00:02:04.866 --> 00:02:07.730
That's right.

00:02:07.730 --> 00:02:10.940
So on that note,
today we are actually

00:02:10.940 --> 00:02:16.340
going to see a lot of
great presentations

00:02:16.340 --> 00:02:18.500
on open data and
research transparency

00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:23.030
and reproducibility across
a broad set of disciplines,

00:02:23.030 --> 00:02:26.720
from astrophysics to
political science,

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:32.252
from American studies
to outcomes research.

00:02:32.252 --> 00:02:36.870
But underpinning all
of these disciplines

00:02:36.870 --> 00:02:39.870
is really the need
for a robust cyber

00:02:39.870 --> 00:02:41.840
infrastructure on campuses.

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:46.440
And as part of that
infrastructure,

00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:48.580
it's not just the
technology itself,

00:02:48.580 --> 00:02:52.290
but it's also access
to skill sets on how

00:02:52.290 --> 00:02:56.880
to use this infrastructure,
as well as the training

00:02:56.880 --> 00:02:59.100
to keep up to speed
with the latest

00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:01.475
and ever-changing
technology trends on campus.

00:03:04.090 --> 00:03:07.000
The mission for the Yale
Center for Research Computing

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:09.640
is to build this
dynamic community

00:03:09.640 --> 00:03:13.600
to support these data and
computationally-intensive needs

00:03:13.600 --> 00:03:16.700
in each of these disciplines.

00:03:16.700 --> 00:03:19.100
Today we welcome you to--

00:03:19.100 --> 00:03:22.100
of course, the Fourth Annual
Day of Data-- but also

00:03:22.100 --> 00:03:24.920
to the official opening
of the Yale Center

00:03:24.920 --> 00:03:27.830
for Research Computing.

00:03:27.830 --> 00:03:31.940
Planning for this center
started about two years ago.

00:03:31.940 --> 00:03:35.100
And today we are officially
open for business.

00:03:38.650 --> 00:03:42.690
As Steve had shown, if we look
at the advancements in some

00:03:42.690 --> 00:03:45.670
of these scientific
techniques-- so, for example,

00:03:45.670 --> 00:03:49.080
the ability to map the
structure of the Zika virus with

00:03:49.080 --> 00:03:53.580
cryo-electron microscopy, or
the ability to create these 3D

00:03:53.580 --> 00:03:56.880
models of ancient Egyptian
artifacts from photographs,

00:03:56.880 --> 00:04:01.680
or if we look at the decreasing
trends in costs in sequencing

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:04.380
a genome-- the ability to
sequence a genome for $1,000

00:04:04.380 --> 00:04:09.150
now versus the $100
million just 15 years ago--

00:04:09.150 --> 00:04:13.350
the complexities surrounding the
computational needs on campus

00:04:13.350 --> 00:04:17.220
are increasing and
advancing as well.

00:04:17.220 --> 00:04:20.231
And so it's soon not
going to be uncommon

00:04:20.231 --> 00:04:21.980
where we're going to
have to transfer data

00:04:21.980 --> 00:04:25.910
at 100 gigabits per
second on campus or store

00:04:25.910 --> 00:04:30.140
multiple petabytes of data
a day, and then archive

00:04:30.140 --> 00:04:36.180
that data to be able to keep up
with these federal mandates--

00:04:36.180 --> 00:04:37.120
Unfunded.

00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:38.096
Unfunded mandates.

00:04:40.970 --> 00:04:43.690
And so it's going to get
trickier and trickier

00:04:43.690 --> 00:04:46.360
and trickier to keep up
with these computational

00:04:46.360 --> 00:04:47.380
environments.

00:04:47.380 --> 00:04:50.680
And that, to me, is why a lot
of these tier 1 institutions

00:04:50.680 --> 00:04:52.930
have-- or are starting to
create-- these research

00:04:52.930 --> 00:04:55.750
computing centers, and why
there is a national dialogue

00:04:55.750 --> 00:04:59.050
at this point on how to
support researchers on campus,

00:04:59.050 --> 00:05:01.510
and what are the skill sets
that are necessary to do so.

00:05:04.600 --> 00:05:07.510
In the last 12
months, the YCRC has

00:05:07.510 --> 00:05:10.720
been involved in a
variety of initiatives,

00:05:10.720 --> 00:05:13.300
from acquiring funding
to refresh hardware

00:05:13.300 --> 00:05:15.130
infrastructure--
the HPC computing

00:05:15.130 --> 00:05:18.300
clusters-- with the Yale
Center for genome analysis,

00:05:18.300 --> 00:05:22.780
to providing dedicated support
to groups like climatology,

00:05:22.780 --> 00:05:27.390
or creating orbital
simulators with astrophysics.

00:05:27.390 --> 00:05:31.410
But when thinking through an
example of what demonstrates

00:05:31.410 --> 00:05:33.570
the broad suite of
services that we offer here

00:05:33.570 --> 00:05:37.770
at the center, what came
to mind is a faculty member

00:05:37.770 --> 00:05:41.690
who recently joined the
Department of Psychology

00:05:41.690 --> 00:05:43.510
and how we handled
that situation.

00:05:43.510 --> 00:05:45.450
So what we did there was we--

00:05:45.450 --> 00:05:48.000
prior to her coming to Yale
from another institution--

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:53.700
we paired her with the research
support group led by Dr. Andy

00:05:53.700 --> 00:05:57.350
Sherman and Dr. Rob Bjornson.

00:05:57.350 --> 00:05:59.800
And they sat with
her and discussed

00:05:59.800 --> 00:06:02.191
what's it going to take to
get your data here to Yale

00:06:02.191 --> 00:06:03.940
and what sort of
computational environment

00:06:03.940 --> 00:06:06.070
are you going to need
to process that data,

00:06:06.070 --> 00:06:07.845
given that some of
it is sensitive data.

00:06:11.510 --> 00:06:15.750
They provided the data
management infrastructure--

00:06:15.750 --> 00:06:20.070
a storage infrastructure
and archive solution--

00:06:20.070 --> 00:06:22.200
to house her sensitive data.

00:06:22.200 --> 00:06:26.010
They provided her with access
to the computing cluster set up

00:06:26.010 --> 00:06:30.040
by the systems administration
team at the Computing Center.

00:06:30.040 --> 00:06:32.130
They've equipped
her lab with access

00:06:32.130 --> 00:06:33.940
to the high-speed
network on campus,

00:06:33.940 --> 00:06:37.260
which allows her to transfer
her data from her lab

00:06:37.260 --> 00:06:40.470
to the compute resources
at a level much faster

00:06:40.470 --> 00:06:43.680
than the standard
network on campus

00:06:43.680 --> 00:06:45.360
They continue to
plan with her what

00:06:45.360 --> 00:06:48.600
her next set of investments
will be on infrastructure.

00:06:48.600 --> 00:06:51.960
And they will be
providing training

00:06:51.960 --> 00:06:55.140
as to how to use
this infrastructure.

00:06:55.140 --> 00:06:58.410
We host a lot of workshops
here at the center.

00:06:58.410 --> 00:07:01.650
Since this past July
1, we've trained

00:07:01.650 --> 00:07:04.290
over 400 people on
workshops such as scripting

00:07:04.290 --> 00:07:11.090
with Python, R, HBC boot
camps, Amazon Web Services.

00:07:11.090 --> 00:07:15.600
And so we continue to build
out that suite of workshops.

00:07:18.360 --> 00:07:21.710
So of course we're involved with
everything computational here

00:07:21.710 --> 00:07:23.550
at the Research
Computing Center,

00:07:23.550 --> 00:07:26.372
but we can also be seen
as an entry point for some

00:07:26.372 --> 00:07:28.830
of the other initiatives-- that
Steve had alluded to-- here

00:07:28.830 --> 00:07:32.580
on campus, such as the
Data and E-Science Group,

00:07:32.580 --> 00:07:35.130
the Research Data
Consultation Group--

00:07:35.130 --> 00:07:36.690
both out of the library--

00:07:36.690 --> 00:07:39.255
ITS Research Technology
Services in ITS.

00:07:42.670 --> 00:07:44.890
We're a nimble group
and a small group,

00:07:44.890 --> 00:07:50.800
but we are complemented by
OIRC Steering Committee.

00:07:50.800 --> 00:07:53.110
Many of you who
are here today, who

00:07:53.110 --> 00:07:56.740
have influenced and provided
insight on the direction

00:07:56.740 --> 00:07:58.720
of some of our initiatives.

00:07:58.720 --> 00:08:01.510
We're supported by our two
faculty co-directors who are

00:08:01.510 --> 00:08:02.190
here--

00:08:02.190 --> 00:08:04.420
Harlan Krumholz
and Daisuke Nagai--

00:08:04.420 --> 00:08:06.580
who consistently
provide me with feedback

00:08:06.580 --> 00:08:09.400
on the pain points on campus
and how we can turn those

00:08:09.400 --> 00:08:10.650
into opportunities.

00:08:13.260 --> 00:08:16.620
And then we've, of
course, had the support

00:08:16.620 --> 00:08:19.402
of the Provost Office
and the medical school

00:08:19.402 --> 00:08:21.110
through Steve Girvin
and Carolyn Slayman.

00:08:21.110 --> 00:08:24.110
And we're grateful for that.

00:08:24.110 --> 00:08:25.280
We've done a lot.

00:08:25.280 --> 00:08:26.960
We know there's
plenty more to do.

00:08:26.960 --> 00:08:30.170
Our office hours are
from 9:00 to 5:00 daily.

00:08:30.170 --> 00:08:32.761
Dedicated support hours with
our research support groups

00:08:32.761 --> 00:08:35.094
are posted on the website,
as are the training seminars.

00:08:37.710 --> 00:08:40.947
We thank you for coming
to today's event.

00:08:40.947 --> 00:08:42.530
I do hope that I get
a chance to speak

00:08:42.530 --> 00:08:45.790
with you throughout the day
or in the coming months.

00:08:45.790 --> 00:08:50.290
And now, as is customary of
the opening of any new center,

00:08:50.290 --> 00:08:52.600
we call Steve Girvin
to do a ribbon cutting.

00:09:07.883 --> 00:09:10.841
Hold it, these things
are [INAUDIBLE].

00:09:16.310 --> 00:09:21.810
So we're happy to have
this fabulous [INAUDIBLE]

00:09:21.810 --> 00:09:25.320
and this center to
help with research.

00:09:30.420 --> 00:09:31.320
Oh!

00:09:31.320 --> 00:09:33.770
[APPLAUSE]

